     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order as written. 25,500 mg a day is about 10 times the maximum metformin dose, and in a 70 kg patient with normal kidney function it could cause life-threatening lactic acidosis. You didn't give a clinical reason for the dose, and I don't know of one that would justify it. Entering it would put the patient at serious risk.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **If it was a typo,** I can enter 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals (2,550 mg a day) right now. I can also enter a lower starting dose, such as 500 mg twice daily.\n- **If you think the dose is intended,** please check it with your attending or a pharmacist first. If they confirm it, it should go through a process with proper clinical review, not be entered by me.\n- **If you're worried about a possible metformin overdose,** that's a toxicology and poison control issue, not a standard order. Poison control is 1-800-222-1222 in the US.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
